          "create_time": 1792878946.259,
          "update_time": 1792878956.155,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "The research has reached a substantial analytical checkpoint: **there are quantitative descriptions of both extreme-time regimes, and exact equations for a smoothing parameter that varies with height and time. The full averaged evolution model remains unfinished.** That is the central message of the [continuation note](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/01_heat_density_asymptotics/notes/15_CONTINUATION_AFTER_MANUSCRIPT_AND_VARIABLE_EPSILON_20261024.md).\n\nThe overall aim is to describe the average distribution of zeros by a smooth density, determine how that density evolves, and eventually solve an approximation with compatible boundary conditions and controlled error.\n\nThe work currently provides three main pieces:\n\n- **Large negative time:** a Gaussian approximation to the regularized density, with explicit errors covering every real height within the stated transverse strips. A stationary profile emerges after rescaling. Letting epsilon track the field’s amplitude changes the correct scaling from \\(T^2\\rho\\) to \\(T\\rho\\).\n- **Large positive time:** an approximately equally spaced, or “clock,” zero population on a window that expands with time. The physical density grows; its rescaled shape becomes stationary. Estimates also control collective spacing quantities for growing blocks of zeros. This remains a growing-window result, rather than a uniform description of the whole height axis.\n- **Variable epsilon:** an explicit prescription preserves the positive-time density accuracy on that window. The new note derives the additional forcing, current and curvature terms required when epsilon varies. In particular, the positive density and the curvature of its potential become different objects.\n\nThese are recorded as **proved-in-note results with same-assistant review** in the [research result summary](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/result-summaries/13_local_heat_flow_geometry_and_rh_equivalent_criteria.md:694). The fixed-epsilon results have been consolidated into a 15-page manuscript; the variable-epsilon extension remains in [Note 14](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/01_heat_density_asymptotics/notes/14_VARIABLE_EPSILON_EVOLUTION_AND_ASYMPTOTICS_20261024.md).\n\n**The main gap is the passage from accurate density snapshots to accurate dynamics.** Averaging introduces correlations and fluctuation terms that are not yet sufficiently controlled. Changing epsilon also introduces time-dependent forcing that can be significant even when its static density bias is small—the distinction emphasized in the repository’s [error-accounting guidance](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/avoid/06_status_stronger_than_the_evidence.md:66).\n\nThe next steps are:\n\n1. **Control the adaptive evolution terms.** Use the recommended explicit \\(p=1\\) regulator and fixed averaging kernels. Bound its averaged forcing and current source, preserving cancellations near zeros, and obtain the necessary time-differentiated approximation estimates. This is the most direct next bounded task.\n2. **Extend the positive-time approximation to larger heights.** Analyze the transition beyond the existing window, where the field’s amplitude changes substantially. Extending the regulator’s formula alone does not extend its error theorem; a new complex-saddle estimate is needed.\n3. **Build and test the reduced evolution.** Once the fluctuation and derivative errors are controlled, formulate compatible boundary data, establish stability, solve the reduced equation, and compare its evolution against the actual theta field.\n4. **Strengthen verification alongside that work.** Obtain independent mathematical review and certified finite-time data showing where the asymptotic estimates become effective.\n\nThe [progress ledger](/Users/ebbaker/Documents/shifted-zeta-positivity/papers/investigations-5/01_heat_density_asymptotics/research-progress/01_AVERAGED_DENSITY_RESEARCH_PROGRESS_20261024.md) accordingly marks the manuscript and bounded adaptive-regulator study complete, while the evolution solution remains gated by those missing estimates. No RH or endpoint-simplicity conclusion is established."
            ]
          },
